Define $\Gamma_{\sigma\mu
u} = g_{\sigma\lambda}\Gamma^\lambda_{\mu
u} = 	frac12\left(\partial_\mu g_{\sigma
u} + \partial_
u g_{\sigma\mu} - \partial_\sigma g_{\mu
u}ight)$. Then $
abla_\mu g_{
u\lambda} = \partial_\mu g_{
u\lambda} - \Gamma^\sigma_{\mu
u}g_{\sigma\lambda} - \Gamma^\sigma_{\mu\lambda}g_{
u\sigma} = \partial_\mu g_{
u\lambda} - \Gamma_{\lambda\mu
u} - \Gamma_{
u\mu\lambda}$. Now $\Gamma_{\lambda\mu
u} + \Gamma_{
u\mu\lambda} = 	frac12(\partial_\mu g_{\lambda
u} + \partial_
u g_{\lambda\mu} - \partial_\lambda g_{\mu
u}) + 	frac12(\partial_\mu g_{
u\lambda} + \partial_\lambda g_{
u\mu} - \partial_
u g_{\mu\lambda}) = \partial_\mu g_{
u\lambda}$, because the other four terms cancel in pairs. Hence $
abla_\mu g_{
u\lambda} = 0$.
